.TH CUT2 1 "February 2025" "cut2 1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
cut2 \- enhanced field and character selection utility with PCRE delimiter support

.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cut2
[\-f LIST | \-c LIST] [\-d DELIM] [FILE...]

.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B cut2
command extracts fields or characters from input files or standard input. Unlike GNU cut, it correctly handles quoted fields, escaped characters, and supports PCRE regular expressions for field delimiters.

By default, fields are separated by one or more tabs or spaces ([\et ]+).

.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-f LIST
Select fields to extract. LIST can be a comma-separated list of field numbers and ranges (e.g., 1,3,5-7). Fields are delimited by the pattern specified with \-d, or by default [\et ]+ (one or more tabs or spaces).

.TP
.B \-c LIST
Select specific character positions. LIST can be a comma-separated list of positions and ranges (e.g., 1-3,5,7-).

.TP
.B \-d DELIM
Use DELIM as the field delimiter PCRE pattern. Default is [\et ]+ (one or more tabs or spaces). The delimiter is interpreted as a Perl Compatible Regular Expression (PCRE).

.SH FIELD LISTS
Field and character lists support the following formats:
.TP
.B N
The Nth field or character (starting from 1)
.TP
.B N\-M
Fields or characters from N to M (inclusive)
.TP
.B N\-
Fields or characters from N to the end of the line
.TP
.B N,M,P
Multiple individual fields or characters

.SH EXAMPLES
Extract the second field from space/tab-separated input:
.RS
.B echo 'John    Doe    30' | cut2 \-f2
.br
Doe
.RE

Extract fields with quoted values:
.RS
.B echo 'John "Doe Smith" 30' | cut2 \-f2
.br
Doe Smith
.RE

Extract fields 1 and 3 from comma-separated values:
.RS
.B echo 'Alice,Bob,25' | cut2 \-f1,3 \-d','
.br
Alice	25
.RE

Extract fields using a regex delimiter (comma or semicolon):
.RS
.B echo 'a,b;c,d' | cut2 \-f2,4 \-d'[,;]'
.br
b	d
.RE

Extract character positions 1-3:
.RS
.B echo "abcdef" | cut2 \-c1\-3
.br
abc
.RE

Extract fields from files with multiple spaces:
.RS
.B echo 'foo  bar    baz' | cut2 \-f1,3
.br
foo	baz
.RE

.SH NOTES
.IP \(bu 2
The output delimiter for field mode is always a tab character, regardless of the input delimiter pattern.
.IP \(bu 2
Quoted fields (using single or double quotes) are preserved as single fields even if they contain the delimiter pattern.
.IP \(bu 2
Escaped characters within fields are handled correctly (e.g., \e" or \e').
.IP \(bu 2
The PCRE pattern must be valid; invalid patterns will cause the program to exit with an error.

.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success
.TP
.B 1
Error (file not found, invalid PCRE pattern, etc.)

.SH AUTHOR
Written by OpenAI.

.SH SEE ALSO
.BR cut (1),
.BR awk (1),
.BR pcre (3)